{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_71","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/tech-design.md | sed -n '555,580p'\"","aggregated_output":"   555\t### Flow 2: Manage Purpose, Access Mode, and Target Ref\n   556\t\n   557\tThis flow covers AC-2.1 through AC-2.4. Purpose and access mode are durable\n   558\tmetadata. Target ref is part of the source definition; changing it marks a\n   559\tpreviously hydrated attachment stale until refresh/rehydration catches up.\n   560\t\n   561\t`SourceManagementService.updateSource` validates the actor, loads the active\n   562\tsource row, normalizes the target ref, and calls `PlatformStore.updateSource`.\n   563\tConvex applies the update atomically. If the target ref changes while\n   564\t`hydrationState === 'hydrated'`, the Fastify-side service includes the requested\n   565\tfreshness transition in the write plan and Convex persists it with the metadata\n   566\tupdate. The resulting row uses `hydrationState: 'stale'` and a\n   567\t`freshnessReason` such as `target_ref_changed`.\n   568\t\n   569\tFor `read_write` sources, the service validates that `targetRef` is a writable\n   570\tbranch ref before persistence. If `targetRef` is omitted for `read_write`,\n   571\tEpic 6 resolves it to the repository default branch before the row is written.\n   572\t`read_only` sources may use branch, tag, or commit refs.\n   573\t\n   574\t### Flow 3: Hydration and Freshness\n   575\t\n   576\tThis flow covers AC-3.1 through AC-3.3. The four canonical states remain\n   577\t`not_hydrated`, `hydrated`, `stale`, and `unavailable`. Pending refresh is not a\n   578\tstate in that enum; it is operation progress metadata returned by refresh and\n   579\tvisible in the client.\n   580\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
